    "q-223098": {
      "dom_id": "step-q223098",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\nT(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩.\n$$\n\nThe unit tangent vector to a curve at an arbitrary point is given above. Find the principal normal vector to that curve at an arbitrary point on the curve.\n\n$N(t)=$\n$⟨$\n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)⟩) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we find the magnitude of ${T}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =2‖⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩‖ \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{2⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩}{2} \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
